WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:06.087
hello-devops now has more than ten YAML files.

2
00:00:06.087 --> 00:00:08.784
Now run it for dev, staging and prod.

3
00:00:08.933 --> 00:00:12.179
The easy way is to copy the folder three times.

4
00:00:12.179 --> 00:00:16.392
Each copy gets its own image tag, replica count and greeting.

5
00:00:16.533 --> 00:00:19.100
Prod also wants a disruption budget.

6
00:00:19.267 --> 00:00:21.175
Then the copies drift apart.

7
00:00:21.175 --> 00:00:24.379
Someone fixes a probe in prod, and forgets dev.

8
00:00:24.533 --> 00:00:28.423
Six months later, nobody knows which differences are on purpose.

9
00:00:29.333 --> 00:00:33.436
There are two popular answers, and they come from opposite directions.

10
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:34.801
Helm is templating.

11
00:00:34.801 --> 00:00:39.544
Write the YAML once, with blanks, and fill the blanks for each environment.

12
00:00:39.733 --> 00:00:41.302
Kustomize is patching.

13
00:00:41.302 --> 00:00:48.216
Keep plain, valid YAML as a base, and describe each environment as a small set of changes on top.

14
00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:51.873
Either way, the result is ordinary Kubernetes YAML.

15
00:00:51.873 --> 00:00:55.005
The API server never knows which tool made it.

16
00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:56.888
Start with Helm.

17
00:00:56.888 --> 00:00:58.792
A package is called a chart.

18
00:00:58.792 --> 00:01:01.580
Its templates are YAML with placeholders.

19
00:01:01.733 --> 00:01:03.467
Each placeholder is a blank.

20
00:01:03.467 --> 00:01:06.192
This one reads replicaCount from the values.

21
00:01:06.192 --> 00:01:09.412
This one reads the greeting, and wraps it in quotes.

22
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.638
The chart's values.yaml holds the default settings.

23
00:01:13.638 --> 00:01:14.668
Two replicas.

24
00:01:14.668 --> 00:01:15.935
Hello from Helm.

25
00:01:16.133 --> 00:01:19.176
Your own values file goes on top, with -f.

26
00:01:19.176 --> 00:01:23.016
The lab's kind-values.yaml changes only the greeting.

27
00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:24.606
Layers stack in order.

28
00:01:24.606 --> 00:01:27.992
When two layers set the same key, the later one wins.

29
00:01:28.133 --> 00:01:32.065
So the result is two replicas, and Hello from Helm on kind.

30
00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:37.645
helm template fills every blank and prints the final YAML, without touching the cluster.

31
00:01:37.645 --> 00:01:39.253
Render before you install.

32
00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:44.377
helm install applies it, and records revision 1 of a release named hello.

33
00:01:44.533 --> 00:01:48.217
Later you run helm upgrade, with just one new setting.

34
00:01:48.217 --> 00:01:49.923
Set replicaCount to four.

35
00:01:50.067 --> 00:01:51.358
Here is the surprise.

36
00:01:51.358 --> 00:01:58.243
When you pass any values on an upgrade, Helm starts from the chart's defaults plus only the values you pass now.

37
00:01:58.243 --> 00:02:01.009
The file you gave at install time is dropped.

38
00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:05.043
Revision 2 has four replicas, and the old greeting is back.

39
00:02:05.043 --> 00:02:08.040
That surprises nearly everyone the first time.

40
00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:12.623
So keep your settings in a values file, and pass it with -f every time.

41
00:02:13.533 --> 00:02:14.634
Now Kustomize.

42
00:02:14.634 --> 00:02:16.600
It is built into kubectl.

43
00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:21.553
The base is your plain k8s folder, the files you already apply.

44
00:02:21.733 --> 00:02:25.729
Every file stays as it is, and you can still read and apply it.

45
00:02:25.867 --> 00:02:29.834
An overlay is another folder, with its own kustomization file.

46
00:02:29.834 --> 00:02:32.394
It points at the base and lists changes.

47
00:02:32.394 --> 00:02:35.337
Think of a sheet of tracing paper laid on top.

48
00:02:35.533 --> 00:02:40.241
The prod overlay moves every object into the namespace hello-prod.

49
00:02:40.241 --> 00:02:41.810
It sets four replicas.

50
00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:44.449
It switches the Service to ClusterIP.

51
00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:49.129
And it adds one file that only prod has, a PodDisruptionBudget.

52
00:02:49.267 --> 00:02:52.174
kubectl kustomize prints the result.

53
00:02:52.174 --> 00:02:53.547
Plain YAML again.

54
00:02:53.733 --> 00:02:57.013
Lift the sheet off, and the base is untouched.

55
00:02:57.013 --> 00:02:58.653
Nothing in k8s changed.

56
00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:01.362
Lay down the dev sheet instead.

57
00:03:01.362 --> 00:03:06.237
Namespace hello-dev, one replica, and a newer image, 1.1.0.

58
00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:08.388
One base, two small overlays.

59
00:03:08.388 --> 00:03:12.365
Applied, they run side by side, each in its own namespace.

60
00:03:14.067 --> 00:03:14.735
Which one?

61
00:03:14.735 --> 00:03:17.876
Most third-party software ships as Helm charts.

62
00:03:17.876 --> 00:03:25.628
For your own app with a few differences per environment, either works, and Kustomize keeps every file as plain YAML.

63
00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:27.309
Helm fills in blanks.

64
00:03:27.309 --> 00:03:29.105
Kustomize patches a base.

65
00:03:29.105 --> 00:03:31.836
The cluster only ever sees plain YAML.
